# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where a book is kept
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule A book is one SQLite database in one known folder
Description Every book is a single file with a .sadb name, and they all live in
Description one folder that the program knows without being told. There is no
Description "where would you like to save this": a book is named, and the name
Description decides the file.
Details The folder is ProgramData\SimpleAccount. It is machine-wide rather than
Details per-user, so the books on a machine are the books, whoever is signed
Details in. As many of them as the user makes can live there side by side.
Details
Details The program makes the folder itself, the first time a book is created.
Details That needs no installer and no elevation: a process running as an
Details ordinary user may create a subfolder of ProgramData and owns what it
Details puts there.
Details
Details Nothing is ever created implicitly. A database is initialised when the
Details user asks for a new book and at no other time, so opening a book that
Details is not there is refused rather than quietly made -- a mistyped name
Details must not look like a book that lost all its transactions.
Examples: BookFile
| Action                        | Name     | Effect                                |
| New book                      | Business | Created, initialised, and opened      |
| New book, the second time     | Business | Refused; that book already exists     |
| Open book                     | Business | Opened                                |
| Open book                     | Typo     | Refused; there is no book of that name |
| List books                    |          | Every .sadb file in the folder        |

Attributes BookFile
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Action    | String |         |
| Name      | Text   | none    |
| Effect    | String |         |

BusinessRule One table per collection, one row per entity
Description The schema is not designed separately from this specification: it is
Description read off it. Every Entity declared anywhere in these files becomes a
Description row, and the Collection it belongs to becomes the table. Its
Description attributes become the columns, with the same names and the same
Description defaults.
Details So there is nothing to keep in step by hand. Adding an attribute to an
Details Entity adds a column; declaring a new Entity that belongs in a book
Details adds a table. The check that the two have not drifted is mechanical:
Details every Entity in these files appears below, in one of the three
Details groups, and a reviewer can see at a glance that none was forgotten.
Details
Details Which group an Entity is in follows from the rule above. Nearly
Details everything is in the book's own file. One thing sits beside the books,
Details because it describes Quicken rather than any book of ours. And what is
Details only ever being looked at is stored nowhere: a statement being
Details imported, a Quicken report being compared against. Those belong to the
Details act of importing rather than to a book.
Details
Details Holdings are the exception and are marked "not yet" rather than left
Details out: investments are carried as balances only in this round, so there
Details is nothing to store per security. The row stays in the table above so
Details that the count of entities still matches and nobody has to wonder
Details whether it was forgotten.
Examples: EntityStorage
| Entity            | Collection          | Table                 | Where       |
| Account           | ChartOfAccounts     | accounts              | The book    |
| Transaction       |                     | transactions          | The book    |
| Posting           | Postings            | postings              | The book    |
| ImportId          | ImportIds           | posting_import_ids    | The book    |
| Holding           | Holdings            |                       | Not yet     |
| PayeeRule         | PayeeRules          | payee_rules           | The book    |
| PayeeSplitRule    |                     | payee_split_rules     | The book    |
| AccountMapping    |                     | ofx_account_mappings  | The book    |
| TransferMatchRule | TransferMatchRules  | transfer_match_rules  | The book    |
| Book              | Books               |                       | The folder  |
| CsvProfile        |                     | csv_profiles          | The book    |
| HeaderMatch       | HeaderMatches       | csv_header_matches    | The book    |
| QifProfile        |                     | qif_profiles          | Beside      |
| OfxStatement      |                     |                       | Nowhere     |
| OfxTransaction    | OfxTransactions     |                       | Nowhere     |
| QuickenReport     |                     |                       | Nowhere     |

Attributes EntityStorage
| Attribute  | Type   | Default |
| Entity     | String |         |
| Collection | String | none    |
| Table      | String | none    |
| Where      | String |         |

BusinessRule What a book's file holds besides its entities
Description Two things that are not entities and are needed anyway: which
Description version the schema is, and the book's own name and fiscal year.
Details The version is read before anything else. A file written by a later
Details version of the program is refused and left untouched, because the
Details alternative is a program guessing at columns it does not know and
Details writing the book back in a shape that loses them. Being told to
Details upgrade is a better morning than finding a silently emptied register.
Details
Details The Book entity is the odd one out: there is no table of books inside a
Details book, and no table of books anywhere. The folder is the collection --
Details one file per book -- and a book is chosen from the file names in it
Details with the extension taken off, so the list is made by looking rather
Details than maintained.
Details
Details What a book stores about itself is a single row in a table of its own.
Details A text file of the same name beside the database would do as well, and
Details the reason to prefer the row is that there is then one file per book:
Details copying it, backing it up or moving it to another machine cannot take
Details half of a book, and there is no second file to fall out of step.
Details
Details Amounts are stored as whole cents in an integer column. A binary
Details floating-point column cannot hold 0.10, and a book that cannot add up
Details is the one thing this program may not be.
Details
Details The settings a book carries go in that same row rather than in a table
Details of name and value pairs. There are few of them, each means something
Details different, and a row of named columns can be read without a join and
Details cannot hold a setting nobody recognises. See the check-number window in
Details ImportOfx for the first one that is not simply descriptive.
Examples: BookInfrastructure
| Table  | Columns                        | Notes                              |
| schema | version                        | One row; read before anything else |
| book   | name, fiscal_year_start, check_window_days | One row; the Book entity's own data |

Attributes BookInfrastructure
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Table     | String |         |
| Columns   | String |         |
| Notes     | String | none    |

BusinessRule How an attribute becomes a column
Description Mechanically, by its type, so that no column is a judgement call.
Examples: ColumnType
| AttributeType  | Column  | Notes                                        |
| Money          | INTEGER | Whole cents; never a floating-point column   |
| Date           | TEXT    | ISO, so it sorts and compares as text        |
| Integer        | INTEGER |                                              |
| Boolean        | INTEGER | 0 or 1                                       |
| Text           | TEXT    |                                              |
| AccountPath    | TEXT    | The path, which is the account's identity    |
| TransactionRef | TEXT    |                                              |
| An enumeration | TEXT    | The value's own name, as this file writes it |

Attributes ColumnType
| Attribute     | Type   | Default |
| AttributeType | String |         |
| Column        | String |         |
| Notes         | String | none    |

$WIP
BusinessRule The QIF profiles live in one file beside the books, under a name
Description Named, so there can be more than one: an export made years ago may
Description have a different date order from one made today, and being able to
Description keep both beats answering the question again from memory.
Details The file is settings.sadata in the same folder, and the extension is
Details what keeps it out of the way: a book is a .sadb file, so the list of
Details books does not offer this one and a stray file is never mistaken for a
Details book. It carries its own schema version, read first, for the same
Details reason a book does.
Details
Details It is made on demand, like the folder. A machine that has never
Details imported a QIF file has no such file and needs none.
Examples: BesideTheBooks
| File            | Holds                     | InBookList |
| Business.sadb   | A book                    | Yes        |
| Personal.sadb   | A book                    | Yes        |
| settings.sadata | The QIF profiles, by name | No         |
| notes.txt       | Nothing of ours           | No         |

Attributes BesideTheBooks
| Attribute  | Type   | Default |
| File       | Text   |         |
| Holds      | String |         |
| InBookList | String |         |

$WIP
